{
  "policy_id": "ref-66-reform-social-housing-law-to-prioritise",
  "party": "Reform UK",
  "policy_title": "Reform social housing law to prioritise local people",
  "outcome": "O15",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O15",
    "direction": "worsens",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "By placing foreign nationals at the back of the queue and potentially evicting existing tenants, the policy risks increasing homelessness among some groups and sharpening inter-group divisions \u2014 though its practical housing effect is limited because most allocations already go to UK nationals. The main caveat is that the policy's undefined terms and legal vulnerability make its real-world mechanism highly uncertain.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether the policy could survive legal challenge, and how broadly 'foreign nationals' would be defined in practice, determines almost the entire magnitude of effect on inter-group relations and belonging.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy would prioritise local people and those who have paid into the system, placing foreign nationals at the back of the queue for social housing.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "foreign nationals must go to the back of the queue"
      },
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy extends to giving foreign nationals currently in social housing a three-month grace period to find private accommodation or face deportation.",
        "evidence_id": "E2",
        "quote": "foreign nationals currently in social housing would be given a three-month grace period to secure private accommodation or face deportation"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Around 90% of new lead tenants in social housing were already UK nationals in 2019/20, and this was similar in 2024/25.",
        "evidence_id": "E21",
        "quote": "around 90% of new lead tenants in general needs social lettings were UK nationals"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Current law already requires legal residence and most non-EEA nationals need indefinite leave to remain to access social housing.",
        "evidence_id": "E13",
        "quote": "Non-EEA nationals generally require indefinite leave to remain (ILR) to access public benefits and social housing via local authorities"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Asylum seekers and those with no recourse to public funds are already ineligible for social housing.",
        "evidence_id": "E14",
        "quote": "Asylum seekers, temporary residents on visas, and undocumented migrants typically have \"no recourse to public funds\" and are ineligible for social housing from local authorities"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Non-UK citizens headed 22% of households assessed as homeless or at risk of homelessness in 2024/25.",
        "evidence_id": "E39",
        "quote": "Non-UK citizens headed 22% of households assessed as homeless or at risk of homelessness in 2024/25"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Forcing foreign nationals out of social housing without alternative provision would increase pressure on the private rented sector and likely increase homelessness.",
        "evidence_id": "E26",
        "quote": "Forcing foreign nationals out of social housing would place additional strain on the already stretched private rented sector, potentially driving up rents and increasing competition"
      },
      {
