      "notes": "All claims are within their stated tiers and the quoted evidence entails each claim as stated. The second claim (E2/three-month grace period) is sourced to media reporting of Farage statements rather than the formal policy text, but is correctly coded 'stated' in the sense of party-stated position, and the distinction is noted in context. The projection claims (E26, E18) use appropriately hedged language ('would likely', 'potentially') and the claims match. The 'paid into the system' claim (E27) is legitimately projected as uncertainty. Tier integrity holds: no projection is dressed as measurable. Magnitude ('minor') is justified given the 90% baseline and legal uncertainty. Time horizon matches. The rationale does not impute motive or use loaded political language beyond describing the policy mechanism. Red Brick is flagged as advocacy and explicitly noted as not driving magnitude. No false balance: the worsens direction is coherent with the surviving evidence. No lever leakage: the analysis stays focused on inter-group relations and belonging (O15), not housing allocation as a policy end in itself. One minor observation: E2 is backed partly by vertexaisearch (a redirect aggregator), but the underlying sources (independent.co.uk, guardian.com, housingtoday.co.uk) are credible media, so this does not rise to blocking."
